WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.600
There's not one connection between&nbsp;
mathematics and music there are many.&nbsp;

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.165
It's got so much mathematics in the rhythms

00:00:07.165 --> 00:00:09.128
the patterns, even the counting.

00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:10.933
The way that frequencies match up

00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:13.520
to produce sounds that we&nbsp;
find pleasing together.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.347
The mathematician Leibniz said that music

00:00:16.347 --> 00:00:20.079
is what happens when we do mathematics&nbsp;unconsciously.

00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:24.125
You can't listen to a piece of Bach without knowing

00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:25.349
that it's mathematical.

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:28.350
The&nbsp;beautiful patterns and symmetries in that music

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.507
if you're listening to a fugue or playing a fugue on&nbsp;the piano

00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:34.198
you'll get a theme that comes in

00:00:34.198 --> 00:00:36.820
and then it comes back but it's upside down, inverted.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.697
Or it might be retrograde, backwards.

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:43.277
People somehow they get put off the idea of mathematics.

00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:47.610
They&nbsp;think mathematics is about numbers and numbers are not creative, they're rigid.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.288
Whereas art&nbsp;is more free and creative.

00:00:51.288 --> 00:00:53.894
The most creative subject for me is mathematic,

00:00:53.894 --> 00:00:58.160
it's just that&nbsp;we're creating with with ideas, with patterns.

